Z||000000||speed racer||09-08-2001||03:35 PM||||What is the best bang for the buck lowering suspension? I want to lower my 86SE 1.5 inches. I only see 1-inch springs at the Fiero Store. What other brand are there for my car and why are they better than the Fiero Stores brand and where can I find them at. How much do they improve your handling of your car? Also how much would they cost.<p>[This message has been edited by speed racer (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||09-08-2001||04:30 PM||||Speed,  Your post has been here a while???<P>Please use the SEARCH----Top right on the page.<BR>type in Springs, Lowering, Low, any of that as a search word, in tech. or gen.<P>There is way to much info on this, it's been talked about over & over again!!<P><BR>DXR_DAD<p>[This message has been edited by DXR_DAD (edited 09-08-2001).]||||reg||3
Z||000002||2birds||09-08-2001||10:52 PM||||If your computer is as slow as mine, you'll be at it for a week for a topic this popular on the Search function.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>84-87 are easy.  88 I have no idea about.<P>I have Suspension Techniques (linear rate)springs in mine.  They were $160 for a complete set from JC Whitney back in '95, still about the same, I think.  Eibach makes progressive rate springs for as low as a shade over $200, last time I checked.  I'd spend the extra $$ if I was to do it again, for a better ride with the variable rate springs.<BR>I have all polyurethane bushings, too and it rides (and handles) like a go-kart.<BR>I think the Fiero Store's springs are Eibach's, but don't quote me on that.  <A HREF="http://www.shox.com" TARGET=_blank>www.shox.com</A>  (RD Enterprises) has the best prices I've seen on Eibach's, and on premium shocks.  Kind of slow, but it was on parts for my T-bird.<BR>Replace your upper ball joints with Moog Problem Solvers. They are cheap (mine cost about $20 ea, try Summit Racing or your local parts store (PartsAmerica, Advance, Pep Boys carry or can get Moog stuff).  The benefit to this is that they are slotted to allow front end camber adjustment, which you will need.  Then find a decent alignment shop that will do it right.||||reg||1
